library(haven)
TEDS_2016 <-
haven::read_dta("https://github.com/datageneration/home/blob/master/DataProgramming/data/TEDS_2016.dta?raw=true")
library(dplyr)
glimpse(TEDS_2016)
summary(TEDS_2016)
dim(TEDS_2016)
When working with the TEDS_2016 dataset, several practical challenges arise. First, many variables are imported as labelled numeric variables because the file comes from Stata. Although they appear numeric, they actually represent categorical responses with value labels, which requires careful conversion before analysis.
Second, the dataset contains missing values in several variables, such as voting behavior and income. In addition to true NA values, some survey responses use special numeric codes (e.g., 8, 9, 98, 99) to represent “no response” or “don’t know,” which must be identified and handled properly.
Another issue is that some concepts appear in multiple forms (for example, both Sex and female, or Age and age), so it is necessary to determine which version is appropriate for analysis.
Finally, many variables are coded numerically but represent categories (such as Tondu or education levels). Treating these as continuous variables without proper recoding could lead to misleading results. Therefore, careful data cleaning and variable inspection are necessary before proceeding with further analysis.
Missing values require careful attention. Several variables, such as votetsai, votetsai_all, and income_nm, contain a noticeable number of missing observations. In addition to true NA values, some survey responses are coded using special numeric values (such as 9 or 98) to indicate “no response” or “don’t know.” These codes must first be identified and recoded as NA to ensure they are treated correctly in the analysis.
For this dataset, listwise deletion is appropriate for many descriptive analyses, particularly when focusing on specific variables and when the proportion of missing data is moderate. For example, when examining voting behavior, observations with missing vote responses can be excluded without affecting the rest of the dataset.
However, for political attitude variables such as Tondu, nonresponse may carry substantive meaning. In such cases, keeping “no response” as a separate category could be justified rather than automatically dropping those observations. Because this dataset is largely categorical and ordinal in nature, mean imputation would not be appropriate. Overall, handling missing values depends on the research question, but careful recoding and transparent reporting are essential before proceeding with further analysis.
